---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/for/beauty";
const title = "SMS apps for Shopify beauty brands: shades, routines and replenishment";
const desc = "Beauty messaging needs to distinguish the product someone bought, the routine they actually use and the subscription they already have. Choose an app by those distinctions before comparing promotional templates.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/for">All store types</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">{desc}</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Build replenishment around the item and purchase type</h2>
      <p>A cleanser, a shade-specific foundation and a discovery kit should not share a universal refill timer. Begin with variant, pack size, quantity, order date and subscription status. Use observed repeat-order patterns as planning evidence, with a skip option for customers who still have product. A sample purchase is not proof that the shopper now follows the full routine.</p>
      <h2>Subscription status must interrupt the promotional flow</h2>
      <p>Shopify Subscriptions documents customer controls to pause, resume, cancel and update subscription details, plus subscription email notifications. Keep those notifications owned by the subscription system. Require your marketing app to demonstrate how a skip, pause or upcoming order affects its replenishment audience. Do not send a one-off discount immediately before an already scheduled refill simply because the last order is old.</p>
      <h2>Shade follow-up is different from routine education</h2>
      <p>A restock alert must identify the requested variant, not merely the parent product. Recheck availability at send time and send the shopper to that shade. Put ingredient explanations, application instructions and bundle comparisons in email, with approved product copy. Route irritation or suitability questions to trained support; do not let a marketing automation infer a medical condition or prescribe a routine from browsing behavior.</p>
      <h2>Proposed demonstration: a kit, a refill and a return</h2>
      <p>These are hypothetical acceptance cases, not merchant results. Buy a discovery kit, then one full-size item: only that item should become a refill candidate. Pause its subscription before the scheduled reminder and check the audience again. Return a foundation shade while its restock message is queued: verify that a support hold prevents a cheerful reorder prompt. Test an email-only buyer separately from an SMS subscriber.</p>
      <h2>How to compare suppliers without a generic ranking</h2>
      <p>Bring the same item, variant, subscription and return cases to Postscript, Klaviyo, Omnisend or Attentive. Ask which fields are native, which require an integration and which need maintained custom work. Demonstrate missing-data behavior rather than relying on a screenshot of a segment builder. Choose a dedicated SMS owner if replies need a specialist queue; favor shared channel operations only if the subscription exits and support holds are visible to that team.</p>
      <h2>Economics for refills and launches</h2>
      <p>Separate full-price refill revenue from discounted kit acquisition and subscription orders that would have occurred anyway. Include samples, bundle discounts, shipping subsidy, message format and carrier charges in the contribution model. Track opt-outs by education, restock and promotion flow. A launch can produce high attributed revenue while pulling forward the next refill, so review the following purchase cycle before expanding frequency.</p>
      <h2>A practical rollout and the email role</h2>
      <p>Pilot one variant-specific restock or opt-in refill workflow after permission records, timezone rules, opt-outs and reply ownership are checked. Add routine education only with approved content and explicit purchase exits. Sequenzy may be considered for the email portion if its current event integration supports those exits; no outcome, pricing or native subscription support is assumed. Maintain one promotion calendar across email and SMS so a refill, a launch and a subscription notice do not compete blindly.</p>
      <h2>Primary reference and scope</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/purchase-options/subscriptions/shopify-subscriptions/manage-subscriptions/manage-contracts">Shopify subscription contract states and customer controls</a>. Checked November 25, 2026. This supports the platform behavior described above, not a live integration test or an app ranking. Channel permission and legal requirements depend on destination and message purpose; this page does not certify compliance.</p>
    </article>
    <p class="mt-10"><a class="link" href="/apps">Explore app reviews</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Email and SMS coordination</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">Consent guide</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
